.TH SET_AT_N 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_at_n \- Retrieve an element from a set by index, with length

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>

.IB char*
.IB set_at_n(const set_t* set, int64 pos, size_t* len);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_at_n\fR function retrieves an element from a set by index, along with its length. It takes a set, an index, and a pointer to a size_t variable, and returns a pointer to the element at the specified index. If the index is out of bounds, the function returns \fBNULL\fR.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB set
The set from which to retrieve the element.
.TP
.IB pos
The index of the element to retrieve.
.TP
.IB len
A pointer to a size_t variable that will receive the length of the element.

.SH RETURN VALUE
A pointer to the element at the specified index, or \fBNULL\fR if the index is out of bounds.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_at_n\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>

int main() {
    set_t set;
    char* element;
    size_t element_len;

    set_init(&set);
    set_insert(&set, "apple");
    set_insert(&set, "banana");
    set_insert(&set, "cherry");

    element = set_at_n(&set, 1, &element_len);
    if (element != NULL) {
        printf("Element at index 1: %s, length: %zu\n", element, element_len);
    } else {
        printf("Index out of bounds.\n");
    }

    set_free(&set);
    return 0;
}
.nef